<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Route 1 &middot; recommended</div>
		<h1>Flash the ready image</h1>
		<p class="lede">One download, everything inside: kernel, hardware fixes, the
		six own applications, browser, mail, maps, photos. About 1&nbsp;GB
		compressed.</p>
		<p><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases/latest">Download the latest image</a></p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>What you need</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>A computer</th><td>Linux, with a terminal. Everything below runs
			there, not on the phone. macOS and Windows have <code>fastboot</code> too,
			but these steps have only been walked on Linux.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>fastboot</th><td>Debian/Ubuntu:
			<code>apt install android-sdk-platform-tools</code>. Arch:
			<code>pacman -S android-tools</code>. Alpine:
			<code>apk add android-tools</code>. Fedora:
			<code>dnf install android-tools</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>A USB cable</th><td>Data, not charge-only. Straight into the
			computer if you can &mdash; hubs cause half the "device not found".</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Bootloader unlocked</th><td>Xiaomi's own procedure, with its waiting
			period.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>U-Boot on <code>boot</code></th><td>Matched to your panel. If this
			phone has never run postmarketOS, do <a href="install-pmos.html">route 3</a>
			first &mdash; only its U-Boot step.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Your data</th><td>Gone. This writes the whole system
			partition.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>Put the phone in fastboot</h2></div>
		<p>Turn it off. Then hold <b>volume-down + power</b> until the screen says
		<i>fastboot</i>, and plug the cable in. If the phone already runs this system,
		<code>sudo reboot-to-bootloader</code> on the phone does the same thing.</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR PC</span></div>
			<pre>sudo fastboot devices
<i># prints something like:  83d9a95b   fastboot</i>
<i># nothing? another cable, another port, and check the phone is really</i>
<i># in fastboot and not just off.</i></pre>
		</div>
		<p><code>sudo</code> because without udev rules the device belongs to root.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>03</span><h2>Flash it</h2></div>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR PC</span></div>
			<pre>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS   <b># catches a truncated download before you write it</b>
xz -d @IMG@.xz

sudo fastboot flash userdata @IMG@   <b># userdata. never boot.</b>
sudo fastboot reboot</pre>
		</div>
		<p>The phone boots to the desktop. First boot takes a minute longer.</p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Log in and change the password.</b>
			The image ships user <code>user</code> with password <code>147147</code>,
			because an image has to have one. Change it: <code>passwd</code>.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>04</span><h2>Then</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Wifi</th><td>Settings, as usual. Nothing is preconfigured.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Updates</th><td><code>sudo apk upgrade</code>. The repository line
			and the signing key are already inside, kernel included.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Getting back to fastboot</th><td><code>sudo reboot-to-bootloader</code>.
			<code>systemctl reboot bootloader</code> does not work here.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="devices.html"><b>Read</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
			<a href="packages.html"><b>See</b><span>What is inside</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
